Implicit vs explicit casting
Up-casting can be implicit - in this case the compiler decides which type is the object.
But it can also be explicit, where we can tell the compiler the exact casting type (for example like (int)(Math.random()) or whatever)
Usually there is no reason to use explicit up-casting.

Down Casting - similar to down syndrome we take a fully functioning thing and downgrade it.
its more complex to achieve but overall its when we assign a sub-class to a super class for example: Animal animal;
Cow c = (Cow) animal;
however we can't do Cow c = animal;

The problems that can occur as a result from downcasting - Since a reference of type  C can potentially be cast to any class that extends / implements C. Such code will always compile.
